<h2>The procedural fault line</h2>
<p>Protest petitions occupy a junction between two cognizance regimes. Under Section 190(1)(b) CrPC the Magistrate takes cognizance on a police report; under Section 190(1)(a) read with Chapter XV, on a complaint. The informant dissatisfied with a police filtering — here, a chargesheet that dropped the appellant and reduced the offence — can persuade the Magistrate to either differ with the report on its own record, or to treat the protest petition as a complaint. Each route has its own discipline:</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Differing with the police report</strong><br/>The Magistrate applies independent mind to the report&rsquo;s material and may take cognizance against persons the police dropped — but works within the report; he cannot add offences the material does not disclose.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&ne;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Protest petition as complaint</strong><br/>Chapter XV governs: sworn examination of complainant and witnesses (Section 200), inquiry under Section 202 where required, witness list before summons in Sessions-triable cases. The investigation record cannot substitute for this evidence.</div>
</div>
<p>The Magistrate in this case did neither cleanly: the order took cognizance &ldquo;on the protest petition&rdquo; for offences as grave as Section 302/34 IPC, with no Section 200 examination, no Section 202 inquiry, no witness list — and with reasoning that consisted of criticising the investigation. The Supreme Court described it as an unwarranted exercise of power vitiating the entire proceedings against the appellant.</p>

<h2>The medical record&rsquo;s role</h2>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s recital of the medical evidence explains why the police filtering was no scandal: the post-mortem found two abrasions on the forehead and one on the elbow; the doctors could not ascertain the cause of death, initially suggesting asphyxia from antemortem hanging; the State Medico-Legal Cell later opined post-traumatic cardio-pulmonary thrombo-embolism. Against that record, the Crime Branch&rsquo;s decision to send up only the husband for Sections 323/504 IPC was an investigative conclusion a Magistrate could lawfully differ from — but only through the correct procedural door.</p>

<h2>Section 528 BNSS: the High Court&rsquo;s duty</h2>
<p>The judgment&rsquo;s second holding concerns the inherent power preserved by Section 528 BNSS (the former Section 482 CrPC). The provision confers no new power; it recognises authority that transcends technical barriers and must be exercised where a trial court acts illegally and without jurisdiction. Telling the accused to seek discharge instead — the High Court&rsquo;s answer — was inadequate where the summoning order itself was procedurally void. Practitioners defending improperly summoned clients should frame such petitions as jurisdictional challenges to the cognizance order, not as invitations to weigh disputed facts.</p>

<h2>Checklist: attacking a protest-petition summons</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Identify the route the Magistrate took.</strong> If the order says cognizance was taken on the protest petition, demand Chapter XV compliance; if on the police report, test whether the report&rsquo;s material supports the added accused or offences.</p>
  <p><strong>Look for the witness list.</strong> In Sessions-triable cases, summons without a list of prosecution witnesses is a flagged defect.</p>
  <p><strong>Challenge early.</strong> A procedurally void summoning order is best attacked before charge; acquiescence multiplies costs and remedies narrow.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. It is not legal advice; the validity of any cognizance order depends on its specific record.</div>
